  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe main focus in the short-term is on thunderstorm chances from this\nafternoon into tonight, and whether storms will reach severe \nthresholds. At 18Z, a cold front was moving into north central KS. A \nfew agitated cu had grown tall enough to produce isolated, small \nshowers ahead of the boundary. SBCAPE has increased to 2000-3000 J/kg\nahead of the boundary and DCAPE is over 1000 J/kg, but deep layer \nshear is less impressive at around 25 kts. This environment is \ndeveloping as expected and continues to indicate downburst wind \npotential with any storms that form today. CAMs still do not show a \nlot of agreement on timing and location of the more robust \nconvection, but thinking is that coverage of storms will increase \nafter 23Z with initiation likely occurring along a line extending \nfrom near Sabetha to Manhattan to Herington. Initial updrafts could \ncongeal into clusters as the entire system progresses eastward across\nthe area this evening. Severe potential should begin to diminish in \nthe hours after sunset in far eastern KS as CIN increases. Most CAMs \nthen show another cluster of showers and storms moving into central \nKS overnight in response to a shortwave ahead of a main wave aloft. \nModels show those storms producing some gusty winds in central KS and\nweakening with eastward extent.\n\nRain showers may linger into the early morning hours of Sunday with \nchances decreasing after sunrise. The cold front should be south of \nthe area by midday tomorrow, so with low clouds and north winds, \ntemperatures will be noticeably cooler (following a six day stretch \nof 90+ highs). Will maintain at least slight chance POPs in east- \ncentral areas through the afternoon with rain possibly clipping the \narea from the south near the stalled H85 boundary.\n\nA cooler air mass fully settles into the area with a surface ridge \nearly next week. NBM has high temperatures ranging from the mid 60s \nin far northern KS to the mid 70s near I-35 Monday afternoon in part\ndue to limited insolation from lingering cloud cover. Tuesday could \nbe the coolest day of the period with highs in the 60s nearly area- \nwide (this is cooler than recent nighttime lows). Temperatures slowly\ncreep back up, but stay on the cool side of average through the \nworkweek. POPs return later in the week when longer range models show\nthe next shortwave aloft possibly impacting the region. Confidence \nis not currently high on that possible round of wet weather.",
